# Soft Delete Pattern

This project uses a "Soft Delete" mechanism for data integrity. Records are rarely physically deleted.

## Database Schema
Models typically have:
```prisma
model Example {
  // ...
  deletedAt DateTime?
}
```

## Reading Data
**ALWAYS** exclude deleted items unless explicitly requested (e.g., in "Trash" views).

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT
const items = await prisma.item.findMany({
  where: {
    deletedAt: null
  }
});

// ❌ INCORRECT (Will return deleted items)
const items = await prisma.item.findMany();
```

## Deleting Data
**NEVER** use `delete()` or `deleteMany()` for standard user actions. Use `update()` to set `deletedAt`.

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT (Soft Delete)
await prisma.item.update({
  where: { id },
  data: { deletedAt: new Date() }
});

// ❌ INCORRECT (Hard Delete - Only for admin cleanup)
await prisma.item.delete({
  where: { id }
});
```

## Restoring Data
To restore an item, set `deletedAt` to `null`.

```typescript
await prisma.item.update({
  where: { id },
  data: { deletedAt: null }
});
```

## Cascading Soft Deletes
When soft-deleting a parent entity (e.g., Category), ensure you check for active children or soft-delete them as well, depending on business logic.
